# Cloudflare reads this from the build output (like public/robots.txt) — from dist/ on the
# app Worker, and from build/ on the design Worker. CORS and the indexing policy are set once,
# site-wide, at the bottom; these three rules carry Cache-Control only.
#
# Everything Vite emits under /assets/ is content-hashed, so the long immutable cache is
# safe — a change ships under a new URL. `embed.js` deliberately sits outside this rule at
# the output root: it is the one stable URL hosts hardcode, and it must stay revalidated.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The two unhashed entry files at the output root (#148). Everything above is
# content-hashed and so may be cached forever; these carry stable names precisely so hosts
# can hardcode them, which means the CDN must revalidate instead.
#
# **This rule is a fix, not a formality.** The file's previous version left `embed.js`
# outside `/assets/*` and said it "must stay revalidated" — but nothing enforced it, so the
# platform default applied, and the default differs per host: `sahajatlas.com` served
# `max-age=14400` while `*.pages.dev` served `max-age=0`. Four hours of freshness on the one
# unhashed file that imports content-hashed chunks is exactly the failure `docs/embedding.md`
# warns hosts about — a stale loader asking for chunk names the deploy no longer has, which
# kills the widget with no fallback — and it was invisible on the host every check runs
# against.
#
# `max-age=0, must-revalidate` rather than `no-store`: a conditional request that answers 304
# is cheap, and it keeps the browser cache useful while making staleness impossible.
/auto.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/embed.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate


# CORS. The widget is embedded in OTHER sites, so everything it loads at runtime is a
# cross-origin request from the host page's point of view. A font is always fetched in CORS
# mode, so without this header the self-hosted Raleway silently fails to render (issue #91);
# a `type="module"` script is fetched that way too. sydevs/SahajCloud#913 depends on a
# library's assets staying readable cross-origin.
#
# Workers static assets add no `access-control-allow-origin` of their own. Cloudflare Pages
# used to add it to every static asset by itself, and served this app until #283, so this rule
# is now the only source either site has — and the reason a rendering requirement is not left
# resting on a platform default.
#
# ⚠ EXACTLY ONE rule may set Access-Control-Allow-Origin. Both platforms apply EVERY matching
# rule and join repeated values with a comma, so a second ACAO rule would serve
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, *` — invalid, and #91 again. `scripts/deploy-headers.test.ts`
# holds this, for every header name, before a deploy. The smoke lane only observes the
# symptom, on two URLs, after one.
#
# Indexing (issue #106). The policy and the robots.txt interaction are written down once, in
# public/robots.txt; what follows is only why this half exists.
#
# A HEADER reaches what a <meta> cannot. index.html's tag covers every ROUTE
# (`assets.not_found_handling` rewrites unknown paths to that shell), but the root entry files
# and the hashed assets are each a fetchable URL with no document to carry a tag. This covers
# all of them.
#
# It cannot leak into a host page's own indexing: X-Robots-Tag governs the URL it is served
# on, and a directive on a subresource says nothing about the document embedding it.
# `noindex` alone, deliberately — no `nofollow`, because the canonicals point at WeMeditate
# and there is no reason to stop a crawler following them there.
/*
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
